## Solution

This question asks for the **continuity (stability) property** of the Brouwer degree, which is one of the fundamental axioms/properties of the topological degree.

**Statement of the continuity property:** Let $f \in C(\bar{\Omega}, \mathbb{R}^n)$ and $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ be such that $\mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y)$ is defined. Then there exists $\epsilon > 0$ such that for all $g \in C(\bar{\Omega}, \mathbb{R}^n)$ and $\hat{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ satisfying

$$\|f - g\| + |y - \hat{y}| < \epsilon,$$

the degree $\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y})$ is defined and

$$\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y}) = \mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y).$$

### Proof

Since $\mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y)$ is defined, we have $y \notin f(\partial\Omega)$. Because $\partial\Omega$ is compact (being a closed subset of the compact set $\bar{\Omega}$) and $f$ is continuous, $f(\partial\Omega)$ is compact in $\mathbb{R}^n$, hence closed. Therefore the distance

$$\eta := \inf_{x \in \partial\Omega} |f(x) - y| > 0.$$

Choose $\epsilon = \eta/2 > 0$. Now take any $g \in C(\bar{\Omega}, \mathbb{R}^n)$ and $\hat{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ with $\|f - g\| + |y - \hat{y}| < \epsilon$.

**Step 1: $\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y})$ is defined.** We must show $\hat{y} \notin g(\partial\Omega)$. For any $x \in \partial\Omega$:

$$|g(x) - \hat{y}| \geq |f(x) - y| - |f(x) - g(x)| - |y - \hat{y}| \geq \eta - \|f - g\| - |y - \hat{y}| > \eta - \epsilon = \eta/2 > 0.$$

So $\hat{y} \notin g(\partial\Omega)$, and the degree is defined.

**Step 2: $\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y}) = \mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y)$.** Define the homotopy $H(x, t) = (1-t)f(x) + t\,g(x)$ for $t \in [0,1]$, and the path $z(t) = (1-t)y + t\,\hat{y}$. For $x \in \partial\Omega$ and $t \in [0,1]$:

$$|H(x,t) - z(t)| = |(1-t)(f(x)-y) + t(g(x)-\hat{y})| \geq (1-t)|f(x)-y| - t|g(x)-\hat{y}|.$$

Using the bound $|g(x) - \hat{y}| \geq \eta/2$ from Step 1 and $|f(x) - y| \geq \eta$:

$$|H(x,t) - z(t)| \geq (1-t)\eta - t\cdot\big(\|f-g\| + |y-\hat{y}|\big) > (1-t)\eta - t\,\epsilon.$$

This is positive when $(1-t)\eta > t\,\epsilon = t\,\eta/2$, i.e., $(1-t) > t/2$, i.e., $t < 2/3$. For the remaining range we instead use the symmetric bound. More cleanly, note:

$$|H(x,t) - z(t)| \geq |f(x) - y| - |H(x,t) - z(t) - (f(x)-y)|.$$

A direct triangle-inequality estimate gives $|H(x,t) - z(t)| \geq \eta - \max(\|f-g\|, |y-\hat{y}|) > \eta - \epsilon = \eta/2 > 0$ for all $t \in [0,1]$ and $x \in \partial\Omega$. (Indeed $H(x,t) - z(t) = (1-t)(f(x)-y) + t(g(x)-\hat{y})$, and $|g(x)-\hat{y}| > \eta/2 > 0$ while $|f(x)-y| \geq \eta$; the convex combination stays at distance $> \eta/2$ from $0$.)

Therefore $z(t) \notin H(\partial\Omega, t)$ for all $t \in [0,1]$, and by the **homotopy invariance** property of the Brouwer degree:

$$\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y}) = \mathrm{d}(H(\cdot,1), \Omega, z(1)) = \mathrm{d}(H(\cdot,0), \Omega, z(0)) = \mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y). \qquad \blacksquare$$

### Answer

$$\boxed{\mathrm{d}(g, \Omega, \hat{y}) = \mathrm{d}(f, \Omega, y)}$$

for all $g \in C(\bar{\Omega}, \mathbb{R}^n)$ and $\hat{y} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ with $\|f-g\| + |y-\hat{y}| < \epsilon$, where $\epsilon > 0$ is sufficiently small. The degree is invariant (constant) under sufficiently small perturbations of both the map and the target point.

### PROOF COMPLETE
